          <toggle-section open="false">
            <h3>Video transcript </h3>
            <p><strong>Abraham:</strong> In a time where almost half of UK residents have purchased at least one smart device since the start of the covid19 pandemic, the threat to users and their networks from cyber attacks has never been greater.</p>
<p>Everyday products such as smart watches, TV's cameras and other smart appliances offer huge benefits but are vulnerable to cyber attack.</p>
<p>To counter this threat the Government is planning a new law to make sure that virtually all smart devices meet new security requirements.</p>
<p>Some of the main points of the law will include.</p>
<p>• “Customers must be informed at the point of sale the duration of time for which a smart device will receive security software updates”</p>
<p>• A ban on manufacturers using universal default passwords, such as ‘password’ or ‘admin’, that are often pre-set in a device’s factory settings and are easily guessable</p>
<p>• Manufacturers will be required to provide a public point of contact to make it simpler for anyone to report a vulnerability.”</p>
<p>Smartphones are also to be covered by the planned 'Secure by Design' legislation. Many people keep their phones for more than four years whilst some companies only offer security updates for just over 2 years. Security updates are important in protecting users against cyber criminals and tech firms need to be more up front about how long they support the security of devices.</p>
<p>Tech associations including the Internet of Secure Things [IoXT] which includes large companies like Google, Amazon and Facebook support this new legislation to boost the security of smart devices.</p>
<p>The government is also looking at research reports providing further evidence:</p>
<p>Development for this legislation has been by the Department for Digital, Culture, Media and Sport [DCMS], in conjunction with the National Cyber Security Centre [NCSC]</p>
<p>“The aim of this Code of Practice is to support all parties involved in the development, manufacturing and retail of consumer IoT with a set of guidelines to ensure that products are secure by design and to make it easier for people to stay secure in a digital world.”</p>

          </toggle-section>
        
      
          
        </section>
        
    
     
    
  
  
